const mindMap = `
# Exercise: Code Generation & Debugging Assistant
## The exercise
### LLM writes process_data
### Tests run it, failures fed back
### Tests expanded: median, non-numbers, empty, bad mode
### Loop: up to 3 retries
## What the run revealed
### Spec never updated: tests are the real spec
### Fix broke a passing test (regression)
### Same prompt, different first draft
### Fragile code extraction, no isolation
### Untested: booleans count as numbers
## Improvements
### Full spec in the prompt
### Full test report, passes included
### Isolated process + timeout
### Stop when progress stalls
## Principles
### Tests define correct
### Feedback must show what to keep
`.trim();
---

<LessonLayout title="5.2 — Exercise: Code Generation & Debugging Assistant">

  <LessonHeader
    num="5.2"
    title="Exercise: Code Generation & Debugging Assistant"
    subtitle="A feedback loop that writes, tests and fixes code, what the course's run revealed, and an improved version to run in Colab."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <LessonToc />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Exercise <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Lesson 5.1's feedback loop, built for real: an LLM writes a Python function
      <code>process_data(numbers, mode)</code>, a test runner executes it, and failing tests go back to
      the model until the code passes.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TB
  T["📝 Task description"]:::step --> G["🤖 LLM writes process_data"]:::llm --> X["🧪 Run the test cases"]:::tool
  X --> Q{{"all pass?"}}:::gate
  Q -->|"yes"| D["✅ Done"]:::done
  Q -->|"no"| F["📋 Feedback: failing tests,<br/>expected vs. actual"]:::fb --> G
  Q -->|"3 retries used"| S["🛑 Stop"]:::warn

  classDef step fill:#f1f5f9,color:#0f172a,stroke:#64748b,stroke-width:2px
  classDef llm  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef tool fill:#ccfbf1,color:#134e4a,stroke:#0d9488,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef fb   fill:#ffedd5,color:#7c2d12,stroke:#ea580c,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">The steps</h3>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 · Task and tests</td><td>A deliberately incomplete spec: <code>mode</code> is <code>"sum"</code> or <code>"average"</code> (default average). Eight tests on ints, floats and negatives.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2 · First generation</td><td>Prompt: "You are an expert Python developer…", code only, inside a <code>python</code> fence. A helper extracts the code and runs the tests.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3 · Expand the tests</td><td>Production feedback arrives (the product manager is on vacation): add a <code>median</code> mode, ignore non-numeric values, return <code>None</code> for empty lists, raise <code>ValueError</code> for an unknown mode. Twelve tests now; <strong>only the tests change, not the spec</strong>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4 · One feedback round</td><td>Send the requirements, the current code and the test results; ask for a fixed version.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5 · The loop</td><td>From scratch: generate, test, and feed back up to three times, stopping when everything passes.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">What the solution run produced</h3>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Run</th><th>Tests passed</th><th>What changed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>First generation, original tests</td><td>8 / 8</td><td>Already filters non-numbers and returns <code>None</code> for empty input</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same code, expanded tests</td><td>8 / 12</td><td>No <code>median</code>; unknown mode returns <code>None</code> instead of raising</td></tr>
          <tr><td>After one feedback round</td><td>12 / 12</td><td>Median added, <code>ValueError</code> raised</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loop · iteration 0 (fresh draft)</td><td>7 / 12</td><td>A <em>different</em> first draft: empty list returns <code>0</code>; raises for median</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loop · iteration 1</td><td>8 / 12</td><td>Empty list fixed, but the <code>ValueError</code> was replaced by <code>return None</code>: a passing test broke</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loop · iteration 2</td><td>12 / 12</td><td>Median and <code>ValueError</code> both in place; also added <code>mode.lower()</code>, which no test asked for</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What the Run Reveals <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>The task description was never updated: median, <code>None</code> and <code>ValueError</code> exist only in the tests</td><td>The model has to reverse-engineer requirements from failure messages. Fine as a TDD exercise; in practice, put every requirement in the prompt.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Iteration 1 fixed one test and broke another</td><td>The feedback listed only failures, so the model didn't know <code>ValueError</code> was already right. Show the whole test report.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The same prompt gave two different first drafts (empty list → <code>None</code>, then <code>0</code>)</td><td>Temperature 0.7: generation varies between runs. Judge a loop over several runs, not one.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The code is pulled out by finding a line that is exactly <code>```python</code></td><td>A trailing space, a <code>```py</code> fence or no fence raises <code>ValueError</code> and crashes the loop.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Generated code runs with <code>exec</code> in the notebook's own process</td><td>No timeout and no isolation: an infinite loop hangs the notebook, a bad line can damage its state.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The final code counts <code>True</code> as 1 (<code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>)</td><td>"Ignore non-numeric values" isn't fully specified or tested: passing all tests ≠ correct.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The model added <code>mode.lower()</code> on its own</td><td>Behaviour no test checks can change silently later.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Reflections <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">What improved across iterations?</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Correctness:</strong> passed tests went 7 → 8 → 12, though not steadily: iteration 1 fixed one case and broke another.</li>
      <li><strong>Error handling:</strong> from silently returning <code>None</code> or <code>0</code> to explicitly raising <code>ValueError</code> with a helpful message.</li>
      <li><strong>Edge cases:</strong> empty lists and mixed types were handled once tests named them; anything untested (booleans, upper-case modes) stayed undefined.</li>
      <li><strong>Readability:</strong> stayed reasonable, but grew by accretion (an <code>elif</code> per fix). Nothing in the loop rewards simpler code.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">How effective was the loop?</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fixed per iteration:</strong> exactly the failures named in the feedback: missing mode, wrong empty-list value, missing exception.</li>
      <li><strong>What persisted:</strong> requirements the tests never stated, and the risk of regressions, because the model only saw what was failing.</li>
      <li><strong>Better feedback:</strong> the full test report (passes included), the complete spec in every prompt, a traceback for crashes, and the history of attempts so the model doesn't repeat itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Compared with traditional debugging:</strong> much faster for small, well-tested functions, and the same discipline (write tests first, change one thing, re-run). But a person understands <em>why</em> code fails and notices missing tests; the loop only optimizes toward the tests it has.</li>
    </ul>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The principle">
      <p>
        In a code feedback loop, <strong>the tests are the specification</strong>. The loop is only as
        good as its tests and as specific as its feedback: say what's wrong <em>and</em> what's already
        right.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Notebook: An Improved Version <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>An improved version that fixes the problems above:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Setup:</strong> Colab's built-in model.</li>
      <li><strong>Spec and tests:</strong> every requirement stated in the prompt, booleans excluded and tested; an exception class as the expected value means "should raise".</li>
      <li><strong>Test runner:</strong> code and tests go to a separate process over stdin, with a timeout; the report lists every test, passes included.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand-written candidates:</strong> a first draft (5 of 11 pass) and a complete version (11 of 11), no model involved.</li>
      <li><strong>Code extraction:</strong> tolerates fence variations and missing fences.</li>
      <li><strong>The loop:</strong> stops when all tests pass, at the limit, or when progress stalls.</li>
      <li><strong>Run:</strong> with Colab's model.</li>
    </ol>

    <NotebookView notebook={debugNb} path="code/course-1/5.2-code-debugging-assistant/code_debugging_assistant.ipynb" />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Running it in Colab">
      <p>
        Run the setup cell first; uncomment a <code>MODEL = ...</code> line there to choose a model. To
        see the loop work harder, choose a smaller model or remove a requirement from <code>TASK</code>
        and watch the tests drive it back.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Checklist <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="Building a code-generation feedback loop:"
      items={[
        '<strong>State every requirement in the prompt</strong> — don’t leave the model to infer them from failures',
        '<strong>Tests cover the edge cases</strong> — empty input, mixed types, booleans, invalid options',
        '<strong>Run generated code isolated</strong> — separate process, timeout; a real sandbox beyond experiments',
        '<strong>Feed back the whole report</strong> — passing tests too, so fixes don’t cause regressions',
        '<strong>Extract code robustly</strong> — fences vary; a parsing crash shouldn’t end the loop',
        '<strong>Stop on stalls</strong> — no progress for N rounds means change something',
        '<strong>Review the final code</strong> — all tests passing proves only what the tests check',
      ]}
    />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'In the solution run, iteration 1 fixed the empty-list test but broke the ValueError test. What most likely caused it?',
        options: ['The feedback listed only failures, so the model didn’t know ValueError was already correct', 'The test runner was broken', 'The model ran out of tokens', 'Python changed how ValueError works'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Show the full report, passing tests included, so the model keeps what already works.',
      },
      {
        q:       'The final code passed all 12 tests, yet process_data([True, 2, 3], "sum") returns 6. Why?',
        options: ['bool is a subclass of int, and no test covered booleans', 'The median logic is wrong', 'sum() ignores booleans', 'The test runner converts True to 2'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Passing every test proves only what the tests check — add the case and state it in the spec.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Why run generated code in a separate process with a timeout instead of exec() in the notebook?',
        options: ['An infinite loop or crash becomes a failed test instead of hanging or breaking the notebook', 'It makes the code faster', 'exec() cannot run functions', 'The model requires it'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Isolation plus a timeout keeps the loop alive; real sandboxing is still needed for untrusted code.',
      },
      {
        q:       'When should the loop stop early, before the iteration limit?',
        options: ['When the pass count hasn’t improved for a few rounds', 'After the first failure', 'Never — always use every iteration', 'When the code gets longer'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'A stalled loop won’t fix itself; change the prompt, the model or the tests instead.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

